// The DISCARD step of error recovery on its own. `@error ";"` says a statement which went wrong may be resumed at the
// next semicolon, so the recovery shifts the error symbol in the state which allows it and then waits for a ";". When
// the token which caused the error is not a ";", it is kept for that state to look at, because the state a recovery
// resumes in is usually waiting for exactly it. Only a parse which fails again on that very same token throws it away,
// which is what an untouched countdown of tokens to shift tells the recovery, and that throwing away is the DISCARD.
//
// The input is two plus signs and a semicolon. The first plus fails at the start, the recovery resumes at the error
// symbol, and the plus fails there too because that state wants a ";". Nothing was shifted in between, so the plus is
// discarded rather than kept, the error symbol is popped, and the recovery starts over. The second plus goes the same
// way. The semicolon then ends the recovery and the parse accepts, so the trace also shows that a discard leaves the
// parser able to carry on.
//
// The scanner is not what this case is about, so its rules are single characters and runs of digits.

@scanner {
    WHITESPACE: /[ \t\n]+/ @skip;

    NUMBER:    /[0-9]+/;
    PLUS:      "+";
    SEMICOLON: ";";
}

@parser {
    statement
        : NUMBER ";"
        | @error ";"
        ;
}
